%[text] # plot
%[text] Plot a quantity of the result against frequency
%[text] Class: result
%%
%[text] ## Syntax
%[text] ```matlabCodeExample
%[text] ax = plot(obj,name)
%[text] ax = plot(ax,obj,name)
%[text] ax = plot(obj,names)
%[text] [ax,lines] = plot(___)
%[text] ```
%%
%[text] ## Description
%[text] `ax = plot(obj,name)` plots the quantity `name` of the `result` object `obj` against `Frequency` on a logarithmic frequency axis, in the current axes, and returns the axes. The axes get a grid, the label "Frequency \[Hz\]" and a y-axis label with the name and the unit of the quantity. Every line gets the readable name of its quantity as `DisplayName`, which `legend` shows.
%[text] `ax = plot(ax,obj,name)` plots in the axes `ax`, as `plot` of MATLAB does. Use `hold on` to add the lines of another result to the same axes, for example to compare two loudspeaker systems.
%[text] `ax = plot(obj,names)` with a string array of names plots several quantities in one axes and shows a legend. The quantities must have the same unit.
%[text] `[ax,lines] = plot(___)` also returns the line objects, one for each name and in the order of `names`, so that you can change their colour or style.
%%
%[text] ## Input Arguments
%[text] `obj` — Result of a loudspeaker system calculation, specified as a [`result`](matlab:open(fullfile(fileparts(fileparts(which('lspsys'))),'doc','result','resultdoc.m'))) object.
%[text] `ax` — Axes to plot in, specified as an `Axes` object. It must be the first argument. Default: the current axes.
%[text] `name` — Quantity to plot, specified as a string scalar or a string array (`names`) with one or more of these names:
%[text:table]{"columnWidths":[240,-1]}
%[text] | Name | Quantity |
%[text] | --- | --- |
%[text] | `"SoundPressureLevel"` | Sound pressure level in dB (`SoundPressureLevel`). |
%[text] | `"splMax"` | Highest sound pressure level that the limits allow in dB ([`splMax`](matlab:open(fullfile(fileparts(fileparts(which('lspsys'))),'doc','result','methods','splmaxdoc','splmaxdoc.m')))). |
%[text] | `"splPowerLimited"` | Sound pressure level at the power limit in dB ([`splPowerLimited`](matlab:open(fullfile(fileparts(fileparts(which('lspsys'))),'doc','result','methods','splpowerlimiteddoc','splpowerlimiteddoc.m')))). |
%[text] | `"splExcursionLimited"` | Sound pressure level at the excursion limit in dB ([`splExcursionLimited`](matlab:open(fullfile(fileparts(fileparts(which('lspsys'))),'doc','result','methods','splexcursionlimiteddoc','splexcursionlimiteddoc.m')))). |
%[text] | `"ElectricalImpedance"` | Magnitude of the electrical impedance in Ohm (`abs(ElectricalImpedance)`). |
%[text] | `"DiaphragmPeakExcursion"` | Peak excursion of the diaphragm in mm (`DiaphragmPeakExcursion`, converted from m). |
%[text:table]
%[text] The names that give a sound pressure level can be combined in one plot. The impedance and the excursion each have another unit and are plotted alone: combining quantities with different units gives the error `result:mixedQuantities`. An unknown name gives the error `result:unknownQuantity`, with the list of the names.
%%
%[text] ## Output Arguments
%[text] `ax` — Axes that contains the plot, returned as an `Axes` object.
%[text] `lines` — Lines of the plot, returned as a column vector of `Line` objects with one line for each name, in the order of `names`.
%%
%[text] ## Examples
%%
%[text] ### Example: Sound pressure level and its limits
%[text] Create a loudspeaker system, give it a closed box of 200 liter and a driver according to the specifications of a B&C 21SW152-8, and plot the sound pressure level that the power limit and the excursion limit allow, with the highest level that both allow. The names that give a sound pressure level are plotted in one axes, and the legend shows their `DisplayName`.
sys = lspsys;
sys.Enclosure = comp.ClosedBox;
sys.Enclosure.RearVolume = 200e-3;
sys.Enclosure.Driver = comp.Driver;
sys.Enclosure.Driver.Re = 6;
sys.Enclosure.Driver.Le = 2.2e-3;
sys.Enclosure.Driver.Qes = 0.38;
sys.Enclosure.Driver.Qms = 6.4;
sys.Enclosure.Driver.Fs = 32;
sys.Enclosure.Driver.Sd = 1680e-4;
sys.Enclosure.Driver.Vas = 200e-3;
sys.Enclosure.Driver.Znom = 8;
sys.Enclosure.Driver.Zmin = 7;
sys.Enclosure.Driver.Pnom = 2000;
sys.Enclosure.Driver.Xvar = 16e-3;
res = sys.createResult;
ax = plot(res,["splPowerLimited","splExcursionLimited","splMax"]); %[output:7e6e6d32]
xlim(ax,[20 1000]) %[output:7e6e6d32]
ylim(ax,[100 150]) %[output:7e6e6d32]
legend(ax,Location="southeast") %[output:7e6e6d32]
%%
%[text] ### Example: Compare two boxes in the same axes
%[text] Plot the electrical impedance of the box of 200 L and, in the same axes with `hold on`, that of a box of 50 L. The second call gets the axes as its first argument; `lines` returns the line, so that its `DisplayName` can name the box.
figure %[output:25c8dbf8]
ax = axes; %[output:25c8dbf8]
hold(ax,"on") %[output:25c8dbf8]
[~,line] = plot(ax,res,"ElectricalImpedance"); %[output:25c8dbf8]
line.DisplayName = "200 L"; %[output:25c8dbf8]
sys.Enclosure.RearVolume = 50e-3;
[~,line] = plot(ax,sys.createResult,"ElectricalImpedance"); %[output:25c8dbf8]
line.DisplayName = "50 L"; %[output:25c8dbf8]
legend(ax,Location="northeast") %[output:25c8dbf8]
%%
%[text] ### Example: Peak excursion
%[text] Plot the peak excursion of the diaphragm of the box of 50 L for the source voltage of 2.83 V, in mm. The y-axis label and the unit come from `plot`; `title` is set on the returned axes.
figure %[output:7a32b2cc]
ax = plot(sys.createResult,"DiaphragmPeakExcursion"); %[output:7a32b2cc]
title(ax,"Peak excursion of the diaphragm, 50 L") %[output:7a32b2cc]
%%
%[text] ## See Also
%[text] [`splMax`](matlab:open(fullfile(fileparts(fileparts(which('lspsys'))),'doc','result','methods','splmaxdoc','splmaxdoc.m'))) | [`splPowerLimited`](matlab:open(fullfile(fileparts(fileparts(which('lspsys'))),'doc','result','methods','splpowerlimiteddoc','splpowerlimiteddoc.m'))) | [`splExcursionLimited`](matlab:open(fullfile(fileparts(fileparts(which('lspsys'))),'doc','result','methods','splexcursionlimiteddoc','splexcursionlimiteddoc.m'))) | [`result`](matlab:open(fullfile(fileparts(fileparts(which('lspsys'))),'doc','result','resultdoc.m')))

